// =============================================================================
// MemQL Spec Reference
// =============================================================================
//
// This file is the source of truth for `spec` syntax. It is NOT loaded by
// the engine (the leading underscore on the path tells every walker to
// skip it).
//
// A spec is an atomic boolean predicate over ONE declared data surface.
// Under the spec/shape binding redesign (epic #2281;
// docs/internal/design/spec-shape-binding-adr.md) the surface is DECLARED,
// not inferred:
//
//   - A spec binds exactly one shape XOR concept in its SIGNATURE:
//         spec <BoundName> <specName> { return <bool> }
//     The bound name resolves through the file-top `use` import; a shape
//     import (`use ...shapes.{ X }`) binds a shape, a concept import
//     (`use ...concepts.{ X }`) binds a concept.
//   - The body is a single `return <boolean expression>`.
//   - The body reads fields by BARE name -- no payload., no <shapeName>.,
//     no <conceptName>. prefix. The signature already names the bound
//     context.
//   - A spec body may NEVER reference actor.* or row.*, and a spec
//     signature may never carry @actor / @row. To predicate on caller or
//     row data, bind a shape that projects it (the only gateway) and read
//     the projected key bare.
//
// Spec vs Trait -- the one-line summary:
//
//                       Trait                         Spec
//   keyword             `trait <name>`                `spec <BoundName> <name>`
//   binding             none (deliberately unbound)   exactly one shape XOR concept
//   body                `return <bare bool>`          `return <bare bool>`
//   classification      row-spec (SQL)                row-spec OR context-spec, by binding
//   author lifecycle    @disabled OK                  @disabled OK
//
// =============================================================================
//   1. Spec-level annotations
// =============================================================================
//
// Every annotation goes above the `spec` keyword. The author surface is
// narrow:
//
//   @description("text")    Human-readable description.   optional
//   @disabled               Lifecycle. @enabled is        optional
//                           RETIRED (epic memql#5375).
//
// The @shape("...") pin is REMOVED (epic #2281) -- the binding moved to
// the signature. The legacy `@use*` family (@useConcept / @useShape / ...)
// was retired earlier (#301). @row / @actor are SHAPE-ONLY markers; a
// spec carrying either is rejected with a migration hint.
//
// =============================================================================
//   2. Concept-bound row spec -- payload props by bare name
// =============================================================================
//
// Bind a concept; read its payload props bare. The engine flattens the
// concept (no `payload.` prefix) and classifies the spec as a row-spec
// (compiles to a SQL WHERE fragment, pushed down to the database).
//
// Every binding below resolves through a file-top `use` import. The parser
// accepts `use` only in the preamble, before the first declaration, so the
// three imports the examples bind (participant concept, actorEnvelope
// @actor shape, agent concept) live here together. Section 4 below declares
// its own `participantRow` @row shape locally rather than importing one --
// see that section for why.

use worker.concepts.{ registration }
use common.shapes.{ actorEnvelope }
use agents.concepts.{ agent }

@description("Matches participants with human participantType")
spec participant isHumanParticipant = row => row.participantType == "human"

@description("Matches participants with AI participantType")
spec participant isAIParticipant = row => row.participantType == "si"

// =============================================================================
//   3. Caller / context spec -- bind an @actor shape
// =============================================================================
//
// A caller predicate binds an @actor shape (the only gateway to the auth
// envelope) and reads the projected key BARE. The engine classifies it as
// a context-spec: it evaluates in-process against the auth envelope, not
// pushed to SQL. Invoke it via the `spec <name>` predicate form for caller-based checks.
//
//     @actor
//     shape actorEnvelope { actor.userId; actor.role; actor.isClusterOwner }
//
// (the `actorEnvelope` shape is imported in the file-top preamble above)

// A ROLE COMPARISON IS NOT AN EXAMPLE HERE ANY MORE (epic memql#5166). This
// slot held `requiresAdmin` -- `role == "admin" || role == "owner"` -- and that
// shape is retired from the corpus, because a slug comparison cannot see a
// custom role at all: it is false for a rank-250 role holding every principal
// verb. Write `@requiresRank("admin")` for a FLOOR on the cluster's ladder, or
// `@requiresCapability("update", "principal")` for a GRANT a role was given.
// Both are validated at load and enforced at execution.
//
// What still belongs in a context-spec is a caller predicate no rank and no
// grant can express -- a question about the actor themselves:

@description("Caller is acting on their own behalf rather than through a delegation.")
spec actorEnvelope isSelfActing = actor => actor.identityId == actor.userId

@description("Caller is the cluster owner.")
spec actorEnvelope isClusterOwner = actor => actor.isClusterOwner == true

// =============================================================================
//   4. Row-metadata spec -- bind a @row shape, read its key bare
// =============================================================================
//
// Row metadata (createdBy, createdAt, ...) is ambient: a spec reaches it
// ONLY by binding a @row shape that projects it. The shape projects
// `row.createdBy` to the flattened key `createdBy`; the spec reads it bare.
//
// `participantRow` is declared HERE, illustratively, for this example only
// -- it is not imported from `dsl/worker/shapes.memql` (the real tree's
// participant projection there is `participantFull`, which does not project
// createdBy). `participant` itself is imported in the file-top preamble
// above.

@row
shape participant participantRow {
  participantType
  row.createdBy
}

@description("Row authored by a specific seed identity.")
spec participantRow isSeedParticipant = row => row.createdBy == "seed" && row.participantType == "human"

// =============================================================================
//   5. Trait -- the deliberately UNBOUND row predicate
// =============================================================================
//
// A `trait` is the one construct with NO signature binding: a generic row
// predicate (over bare payload fields) reusable across any concept that
// carries the field. It is validated against the concrete concept at the
// CALL SITE (a query filter, or composition into a bound spec). It may not
// reference row.* or actor.* (it binds no gateway shape). The tree's trait
// library lives under `dsl/<ns>/traits.memql`.

@description("Matches records with active == true.")
trait isActiveRecord = row => row.active == true

@description("Matches records with deleted != true.")
trait isNotDeleted = row => row.deleted != true

@description("Matches records with status == 'active'.")
trait statusIsActive = row => row.status == "active"

// =============================================================================
//   6. Classification follows the BINDING (not the body)
// =============================================================================
//
//   concept binding, or @row shape binding, or trait
//     -> ROW-SPEC. Compiles to a SQL WHERE fragment. Pushed down to the
//        database. Composable in a query filter clause:
//
//            filter <expr> && specName
//
//   @actor shape binding
//     -> CONTEXT-SPEC. Evaluates in-process against the auth envelope.
//        Invoked via the `spec <name>` predicate form.
//
// The old "mixed body" rejection is obsolete: one binding = one surface,
// so a body can no longer mix row + caller references by construction. To
// predicate on BOTH row and caller data, compose a row-spec (in the query
// filter) with a context-spec (via `spec <name>`) at the call site.

// =============================================================================
//   7. Access legality (the whole contract in one table)
// =============================================================================
//
//   Accessor              In a shape body         In a spec body
//   payload props (bare)  yes                     yes (bare)
//   row.*                 yes, with @row          never -- bind a @row shape
//   actor.*               yes, with @actor        never -- bind an @actor shape
//
// =============================================================================
//   8. Spec body operator vocabulary
// =============================================================================
//
// Comparison operators: == != > >= < <=
// Logical operators:    && || (specs refuse `!` -- write `!=` instead)
// Set operators:        in (X, Y, Z)
// Prefix operator:      field startsWith "lit" / ["a", "b"] / args.x  (ANY of; an empty list and a blank prefix match nothing, memql#4208)
// Null check:           field == null  /  field != null
// Parentheses:          (expr1) || (expr2 && expr3)
//
// (the `agent` concept is imported in the file-top preamble above)

@description("Rich predicate showing the operator surface (concept-bound).")
spec agent richPredicate = row => row.role == "assistant"
                               && (row.roleSlug == "it-support" || row.roleSlug == "customer-service")
                               && row.kind != "system"
                               && row.colorIndex >= 3
                               && row.active == true

// =============================================================================
//   9. Calling specs
// =============================================================================
//
//   - From a query's filter clause: list the spec/trait name alongside
//     other predicates, joined with `&&`:
//
//         use worker.shapes.{ registrationFull }
//         use worker.specs.{ isOnlineRegistration }
//
//         query participant activeHumanParticipants {
//           args { partitionId string @required }
//           filter partitionId == args.partitionId && isHumanParticipant
//           shape  participantFull
//         }
//
//   - Via `spec <name>`: a caller-context boolean check. A context-spec
//     (an @actor-shape binding) is the canonical in-process gate (e.g. the
//     per-row-authz admin gate). The decision-policy tier that once hosted
//     these in a `policy` body is retired (#984); the live `policy`
//     construct is AI provider-selection only.
//
//   - From Go: engine.EvaluateSpec(ctx, "specName") evaluates a
//     context-spec against the request's auth context. Row-specs cannot be
//     called this way -- they require a row to evaluate.
//
// =============================================================================
//   10a. Lifecycle annotations ACCEPTED on specs
// =============================================================================
//
//   @disabled  (and the RETIRED @enabled)
//     Specs accept the same lifecycle flags as functions / builtins /
//     prompts / traits. @enabled was the explicit-on default and is RETIRED
//     in epic memql#5375 -- a no-op that read like a switch. It registered the
//     spec; @disabled parses it but skips registration so it can be
//     re-enabled later without deleting it.
//
// =============================================================================
//   10b. EXPLICITLY REJECTED on specs
// =============================================================================
//
//   @shape("...")
//     Removed (epic #2281). Bind the shape/concept in the signature
//     instead: `spec <boundName> <name> { return <bool> }`.
//
//   @row / @actor
//     Shape-only ambient gateways. A spec/trait may not carry them; bind a
//     @row / @actor shape and read its projected key bare.
//
//   @useConcept(...) / @useShape(...) / any other @use<X>(...)
//     Retired (#301). Cross-file constructs are pulled via a file-top
//     `use <module>.{ ... }` import; the binding is the signature.
//
// =============================================================================
//   11. Retired forms
// =============================================================================
//
// The struct form `spec <BoundName> <name> { return <bool> }` is the only
// spec declaration the parser accepts. Both of these are rejected:
//
//     // NOT VALID -- the bare-expression body is retired; needs `return`
//     // and a signature binding.
//     spec isActive { payload.active == true }
//
//     // NOT VALID -- the receiver-function form is retired.
//     func (Spec) isActive(_ any) bool { return payload.active == true }
